  "text": "In order to solve the given problem, we need to determine the smallest possible value of the degree of the polynomial \\( f(x) \\) given the functional equation \\( f(x) \\sin (2x) = g(x) \\sin (3x) \\) for all \\( x \\). We can proceed with the following steps:\n\n1. Consider the given functional equation: \\( f(x) \\sin (2x) = g(x) \\sin (3x) \\).\n2. Determine the degree of the polynomials on both sides of the equation.\n3. Use the properties of trigonometric functions and polynomials to find the smallest possible degree of \\( f(x) \\).\n\nLet's start by equating the degrees of the terms on the left-hand side and right-hand side of the equation. We know that \\(\\sin(2x)\\) is a polynomial of degree 0, and \\(\\sin(3x)\\) is also a polynomial of degree 0. Since the equation \\( f(x) \\sin (2x) = g(x) \\sin (3x) \\) holds for all \\( x \\), the polynomial degrees of the left-hand side and right-hand side must be equal.\n\n1. The degree of \\( f(x) \\sin (2x) \\) includes the degree of \\( f(x) \\) multiplied by the degree of \\(\\sin (2x)\\), which is 0.\n2. Similarly, the degree of \\( g(x) \\sin (3x) \\) includes the degree of \\( g(x) \\) multiplied by the degree of \\(\\sin (3x)\\), which is 0.\n\nThus, the degree of \\( f(x) \\) must be equal to the degree of \\( g(x) \\) to satisfy the given equation. However, the value of the degree of \\( f(x) \\) must be such that the polynomial \\( f(x) \\) is non-constant.\n\nFor a small non-constant polynomial, the degree can be any positive integer other than zero (which would make the polynomial zero). The smallest possible value of the degree a non-zero polynomial can have is 1, which gives us the smallest non-constant polynomial.\n\nThus, the smallest possible value of the degree of \\( f(x) \\) is \\(\\boxed{1}\\).",
